Z||000000||deceler8||01-18-2000||03:57 PM||||My inductive timing light bit the dust prior to buying my '86 2m4. I do still have one of those direct hookup timing lights (I have it for my 1951 Chevy pickup)...it has the "spring" that goes between the plug and the timing light pickup lead.<P>I tried timing my car using this light and the engine seems to misfire with the light hooked up, so I really don't know if I have the car timed right. <P>Can I use this type of timing light to accurately time my car (and does anyone have a suggestion as to how) or would it be better if I just spent the $$ and got another inductive light ?||||reg||10
Z||000001||DRH||01-18-2000||04:15 PM||||I've used one of those old style lights for years. I bought it when I was 16, I am now 40. It does seem to cause some misfiring especially on "newer" cars. The setting should still be accurate. The light is just "stealing" the spark for that cylinder. Play around with the spring and clip. Keep it as far as possible from surrounding metal. I have had some luck with connecting at the dist. end on some cars. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||dogtired||01-18-2000||04:20 PM||||Hopefully someone that knows will answer your question? I Don't   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> But I would like to add onto your question??<BR>Can an older type timing light be coverted to the inductive style???<BR>Is it just a matter of changing the WIRE???   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Or is there internal electronics that are different?<p>[This message has been edited by dogtired (edited 01-18-2000).]||||reg||6
